 "response": "Bitron's budget is now **$2533K**. I worked this out by tracing every Finance Office record from #0001 to #1600 by hand. The result could be slightly off if I made a slip, though I caught and fixed two missed withdrawals along the way.\n\n**Method**\n1. Messages from anyone other than the Finance Office were ignored. So were Finance Office records that don't change the budget, such as cost-center changes, receipts and audit notes.\n2. Each withdrawal chain was resolved from its last link backward. The last withdrawal in a chain always applies. It cancels the one before it, which then cannot cancel anything, and so on down to the original update. Examples:\n   - **#0003 ($441K decrease):** chain #0851 \u2192 #0794 \u2192 #0362 \u2192 #0149 \u2192 #0018 \u2192 #0008 \u2192 #0004. Resolving it leaves #0004 in force, so #0003 is cancelled.\n   - **#0017 ($67K increase):** #0667 withdraws #0036, so #0036 no longer applies and #0017 stands.\n   - **#0359 ($326K increase):** cancelled by #1090.\n   - **#0918 ($436K increase):** cancelled by #1579.\n   - **#1318 ($474K decrease):** #1580 withdraws #1542, so #1318 stands.\n3. Starting from the $2470K set in #0001, every update that was not cancelled was applied in order.\n\n**Running total at a few checkpoints**\n| After record | Budget |\n|---|---|\n| #0344 | $1982K |\n| #0850 | $3376K |\n| #1316 | $3638K |\n| #1600 | $2533K |\n\nANSWER: $2533K",
